{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are generally too high vs realized prices across most horizons (often by ~1–4 €/t), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction/lag: when the recent 30d history is falling, the model does not fall fast enough (slope too shallow). Increase negative drift for the first ~5–15 days after a clear decline regime.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts upward too early/too strongly after declines, while realized prices keep grinding lower. Reduce upward mean-reversion strength in the first ~2–3 weeks following a selloff.",
    "Late-horizon rebound overshoot: toward days ~20–30 the model often projects a rebound that is larger than realized (and sometimes wrong direction). Damp long-horizon rebounds (shrink deviations from a flat/continuation baseline by ~30–50%).",
    "Volatility/turning-point smoothing: the model misses deeper troughs (local minima) and tends to smooth the path. Add an asymmetric correction that allows larger downside moves than upside moves during bearish regimes."
  ]
}
